-- Quest — assertions about the meadow, and about the state that outlives it.
--
-- Written in the relative vocabulary wherever the answer depends on the screen
-- and in absolute cells wherever it depends on the level: the meadow is 96x28
-- on every console, so "the pit at column 20" means the same thing everywhere,
-- while where the coin counter sits does not.
--
-- Every case opens with `press a`, because every game opens on its title
-- screen. That is one line of ceremony in exchange for the title screen being
-- part of what the suite checks rather than something it routes around.
--
-- One thing the DSL cannot do is hold a direction *and* press a button, so a
-- jump here is `press a` on its own with a `hold` either side of it. The hero
-- keeps the upward velocity across the release, which is what makes that a jump
-- to the right rather than two separate moves — but it also means the routes
-- these cases can take are the ones a player would find clumsy. Everything past
-- the first pit is checked by playing it, not from here.

test the game starts on the title screen and a leads into the meadow
press a
play 2 ticks
expect scene play1
expect lives.value = 3
expect coins.value = 0
expect power.value = 0
expect tokens.value = 0

test the hero falls onto the meadow floor and stays on it
press a
play 2 seconds
expect footing.value > 0
expect p1.bottom = 24
expect p1.ydirection <= 0.05

-- The pits are six cells wide for exactly this reason: a hero is two cells tall
-- and a landing surface is one, so a narrower gap lets it catch the far lip on
-- the way past instead of falling clear, and a pit you can hang on is not a pit.
-- The scene, not the power-up: what happens after the restart is a second run
-- at the same level, and where it has got to when the script stops depends on
-- the console's tick rate.
test running blindly into the first pit costs a life and restarts the level
press a
hold right for 3.33 seconds
expect lives.value = 2
expect scene play1

test a jump carries the hero over the first pit and keeps the life
press a
hold right for 1.42 seconds
press a
hold right for 1.5 seconds
expect p1.x > 26
expect lives.value = 3
expect p1.bottom = 24

-- The mushroom is on the ground before the first pit, because a power-up that
-- has to be platformed for is one most players never see.
test the mushroom is picked up on the way and survives the pit
press a
hold right for 1.42 seconds
expect power.value = 1
press a
hold right for 1.5 seconds
expect power.value = 1

-- Coins are objects rather than tiles, because a tile has no `visible` and so
-- could not be collected, and the counter they feed lives in `title` — which is
-- what lets it survive a level and makes reaching `title` a new game.
test a collected coin reaches the counter that outlives the level
press a
hold right for 1.42 seconds
press a
hold right for 1.5 seconds
press a
hold right for 0.75 seconds
press a
hold right for 0.75 seconds
expect coins.value > 0
expect sc1.value = coins.value

test the hud counters ride the camera rather than the world
press a
hold right for 2 seconds
expect sc1.x = camera.x + 1
expect sc1.y = camera.y + 1
expect sl1.x = camera.x + screenwidth - 3
expect sl1.y = camera.y + 1
expect sl1.value = lives.value

-- Three lives and three pits. The last one is what `lost` is for, and the rule
-- that sends the game there is a guarded `always` rather than a `reaches`,
-- because the trigger has to name no object to run in every scene.
test running out of lives ends the game
press a
hold right for 3.33 seconds
hold right for 3.33 seconds
hold right for 3.33 seconds
play 10 ticks
expect scene lost
expect lives.value < 1
